The <alkene> undergoes concerted <oxygen> transfer from the terminal peroxide <oxygen> of <mCPBA>. In the cyclic <electron>-flow description, the <alkene> pi bond begins one C–O bond, the peroxide <oxygen> supplies the second, O–O breaks, and <proton> transfer/carboxyl rearrangement gives m-chlorobenzoic acid. Both C–O bonds are installed from the same <alkene> face, so the <alkene>'s relative substituent geometry is retained in the <epoxide>. There is no freely rotating carbocation intermediate.

The neighboring free OH <hydrogen>-bonds to the approaching peracid and favors a cyclic, organized transition-state geometry. **<oxygen> is delivered from the OH-directed face, giving the indicated syn relationship between <epoxide> <oxygen> and the allylic hydroxyl.** In the supplied projection, the <epoxide> C–O bonds and the original C–OH bond are on the rear/hashed face. Steric interactions still influence the preferred <hydrogen>-bonded conformer, but “attack from the least hindered face” alone misses the positive directing role of OH. The distant benzyl <ether> is not the directing free hydroxyl.

The classical experimental comparison of allylic alcohols and protected derivatives established this directing principle; it supports the mechanistic interpretation without itself measuring the selectivity of this acyclic substrate.<376. Aspects of stereochemistry. Part I. Stereospecificity in formation of epoxides from cyclic allylic alcohols by H. B. Henbest and R. A. L. Wilson>[1957, pp. 1958–1965, hydroxyl-directed peracid epoxidation]{ref}

\Image[paper-1-protection.png]
{title=Hydroxyl-directed oxygen transfer and stereoretentive acetonide formation}

= b
{parent=B4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Acid protonates one methoxy group of <2,2-dimethoxypropane>. <methanol> departure gives the oxocarbenium species $\mathrm{Me_2C(OMe)^+}$, which is captured by one diol <oxygen>. After <proton> transfer, <protonation> and loss of the remaining methoxy group generate a second oxocarbenium center. Intramolecular attack by the other diol <oxygen> closes the five-membered ring; <deprotonation> regenerates acid. The net <transacetalization> exchanges two methoxy groups for the two diol oxygens, releasing two <methanol> molecules.

**The two starred <carbon> centers retain their original configurations, and the two <carbon> side chains on the <acetonide> are trans.** No C–O bond at either original <stereocenter> is broken; reaction takes place at the reagent-derived CMe$_2$ acetal <carbon>. The apparent change in wedge/dash presentation comes from redrawing the backbone into the five-membered ring, not inversion at a starred center. In the planar pentagon used in the diagram, draw the alkynyl-bearing side chain behind and the $\mathrm{CH_2OPMB}$ side chain in front. The unstarred methyl-bearing center also retains its <configuration>. The acetal <carbon> itself is not stereogenic because its two methyl substituents are identical.

= c
{parent=B4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The reacting <alkene> pi bond and two Os=O units undergo a concerted cycloaddition to give a five-membered <cyclic osmate ester>. Both new <carbon>–<oxygen> bonds form on the same face. Hydrolysis and <proton> transfer cleave the Os–O attachments, releasing the vicinal diol and a reduced osmium-containing species.

**This is syn <dihydroxylation> of the terminal <alkene>.** The terminal <carbon> becomes $\mathrm{CH_2OH}$, and the adjacent <carbon> becomes $\mathrm{CH(OH)}$; the remote vinyl <chloride> remains as drawn. The protected region denoted by $R$ is a spectator for this local mechanism. No explanation of <alkene> chemoselectivity is required here. Since no terminal reoxidant is specified, do not invent a catalytic regeneration step for Os(VIII); osmium reduction and product release suffice for the stated transformation.

= d
{parent=B4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

The free propargylic OH first reacts with an aluminium hydride, releasing <hydrogen> and forming an O–Al bond. Coordination of the nearby <alkyne> to this aluminium center organizes a five-membered O–C–C–C–Al unit. Hydride addition from the opposite side of the activated <alkyne> gives a cyclic <alkenyl aluminate>: aluminium bonds to the distal <alkyne> <carbon>, while hydride is delivered to the <carbon> nearer $\mathrm{CH_2O}$.

**The key step is directed trans <hydroalumination>.** The new C–H and C–Al bonds are anti. Aqueous workup protonolyzes C–Al with retention of its <alkene> geometry and hydrolyzes the O–Al attachment. Replacing that C–Al bond by C–H therefore produces the **E allylic alcohol**, with $\mathrm{CH_2OH}$ and the rest of the chain on opposite <alkene> sides. A generic syn <hydroalumination> drawing followed by simple <protonation> would predict the wrong geometry.

The free OH provides the directing tether; the silyl-protected <oxygen> is not equivalently available. Primary <electrophile>-trapping studies of <Red-Al>-treated <propargylic alcohols> support the alkenyl-alanate description.<trans-hydroalumination-alkylation-one-pot-synthesis-of-trisubstituted-allylic-alcohols-by-neil-f-langille-and-timothy-f-jamison>[2006, pp. 3761–3764, trans-hydroalumination and trapping of alkenyl alanates]{ref}

\Image[paper-1-osmate-aluminate.png]
{title=Cyclic intermediates in osmium dihydroxylation and directed trans hydroalumination}

= e
{parent=B4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

The alcohol <oxygen> attacks <sulfur> of <methanesulfonic anhydride>. Cleavage of the anhydride S–O bond releases methanesulfonate, and the <amine> base removes the O–H <proton>, giving the corresponding <mesylate>. This activation preserves the substrate C–O bond while replacing the poor hydroxide leaving group by $\mathrm{OSO_2Me}$.

The base then removes a <hydrogen> from the adjacent methylene on the side toward the vinyl <chloride>. In a favorable antiperiplanar arrangement, C–H <electrons> form the new C=C bond while the C–OMs bond breaks. **An <E2 elimination> creates the <alkene> between the former alcohol <carbon> and that methylene.** It links the two neighboring unsaturated regions into the extended conjugated triene segment, while the pre-existing <carbon> skeleton and vinyl <chloride> are retained.
$$
\mathrm{{-}C(Me){=}CH{-}CH(OH){-}CH_2{-}CH{=}C(Cl)R}
\longrightarrow
\mathrm{{-}C(Me){=}CH{-}CH{=}CH{-}CH{=}C(Cl)R}.
$$
The left-hand polyenoate portion is unchanged. The base is <diisopropylethylamine>; it accepts acid during <mesylation> and promotes elimination. The product's E/Z mixture concerns the newly formed <alkene>, and no single geometry needs to be assigned.

= B5
{c}
{parent=Paper 1}
