= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

**A: <aldehyde> <oxygen> is the best acceptor; there is no conventional strong O–H or N–H donor.** The N-methylpyrrole <nitrogen> contributes its <lone pair> to aromatic bonding and is not an ordinary <amine> acceptor. A formyl C–H or ring C–H can participate in weak contacts, but is not comparable to the donors in B and C.

**B: <pyridine> <nitrogen> is the best acceptor and the phenolic OH is the best donor.** Its <oxygen> can accept more weakly, but <nitrogen> provides the primary basic site in the depicted hydroxypyridine form.

**C: lactam <carbonyl> <oxygen> is the best acceptor and the <amide> N–H is the donor.** <amide> <nitrogen> is not a useful acceptor because its <lone pair> is delocalized toward the <carbonyl>.

**D: the secondary <amine> <nitrogen> is the best acceptor, and its N–H is the only conventional donor, but a weak one here.** Its <ketone> <oxygen> is also an acceptor, although weaker than the <amine> site in this comparison. These assignments distinguish functional-group identity and lone-pair availability, rather than counting heteroatoms.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

4-Fluorophenol probes acceptor strength because it donates its OH <hydrogen>. The qualitative order is **C $>$ D $>$ B $>$ A**: the <electron>-rich lactam <oxygen> is the strongest acceptor here, the secondary <amine> is next, <pyridine> <nitrogen> is intermediate, and <aldehyde> <oxygen> is weakest. Resonance donation from <amide> <nitrogen> increases the lactam <oxygen>'s basicity relative to an ordinary <aldehyde> or <ketone> <oxygen>. The N-methylpyrrole site in A does not add an independent <amine>-like acceptor.

N-Methylpyrrolidin-2-one is a <carbonyl> acceptor without N–H, so it instead probes the solute's donor ability. **B $>$ C $\gg$ A and D**: B's phenolic OH is a strong donor, the lactam N–H of C is appreciable, A lacks a conventional donor, and D's neutral secondary-<amine> N–H is weak. The <amine> can be a good acceptor and a poor donor at the same time; the two properties are not opposites on one scale.

These are association equilibria in cyclohexane, where solvent competition for <hydrogen bonds> is weak. The quoted constants should be compared within each probe series, not treated as solvent-independent measures. At 298 K, as an illustrative common temperature, a tenfold increase in $K_a$ corresponds to a binding free-energy advantage $RT\ln10\simeq5.7\ \mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$. This does not imply the experiment's temperature was specified, nor that every difference is the energy of one isolated <hydrogen bond>.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

**C is the best geometrical match for a strongly bound 1:1 complex with E.** Both have an adjacent <amide> donor–acceptor edge. Arrange them head-to-tail so that the N–H of E donates to the <carbonyl> <oxygen> of C, and the N–H of C donates to the <carbonyl> <oxygen> of E. This closes the familiar two-<hydrogen>-bond cyclic <amide> motif, illustrated below.

C combines a strong acceptor with a useful donor in the correct spacing. B has a stronger individual donor, but its <pyridine> N and OH are separated around the ring and do not reproduce the compact adjacent <amide> edge of E. A lacks a matching donor, and D's N–H is weak and has the wrong geometry for the same <amide>–<amide> motif. **Site strength and spatial complementarity must be considered together**; multiplying two isolated probe constants is not a reliable prediction of the complete association equilibrium. Self-association of C or E can compete and must be included in a quantitative binding analysis.

\Image[paper-1-recognition.png]
{title=Complementary amide hydrogen bonds, Watson–Crick pairs and guanine recognition}

= b
{parent=B5}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Use the standard Watson–Crick edges, with the glycosidic attachments at purine N9 and pyrimidine N1. The A–T pair has **two <hydrogen bonds>**: <adenine> N6–H donates to <thymine> O4, and <thymine> N3–H donates to <adenine> N1. The C–G pair has **three**: <cytosine> N4–H donates to <guanine> O6, <guanine> N1–H donates to <cytosine> N3, and <guanine> N2–H donates to <cytosine> O2. The middle and right panels above show these atoms and the sugar-attachment directions.

The major-groove side is the edge toward purine N7 and toward <thymine> O4/its methyl group or <cytosine> N4. The minor-groove side is toward purine N3 and <adenine> C2–H or <guanine> N2, and toward pyrimidine O2 and the sugar attachments. Groove labels refer to the exposed outer edges of the paired bases; the <hydrogen bonds> joining the bases are not themselves empty grooves. The resulting patterns differ even when the bases remain paired inside duplex <DNA>.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

At ordinary physiological pH, the <arginine> side chain is predominantly <guanidinium>. Its N–H groups are donors, and its positive charge can reinforce favorable electrostatic interactions. **<guanine> exposes adjacent O6 and N7 acceptors in the <major groove>**, allowing two <guanidinium> N–H donors to make a paired interaction without breaking Watson–Crick pairing. The diagram places this <arginine> contact on the exposed <guanine> edge, not between the paired bases.

<adenine> also exposes N7, but at the corresponding neighboring position it has the exocyclic N6 amino donor rather than a <carbonyl> acceptor. It therefore cannot make the same donor-to-two-acceptors match with <guanidinium>. A single N7 contact would be less discriminating. The classic structural recognition analysis explicitly proposed paired <arginine>–<guanine> contacts in the <major groove>.<Sequence-specific recognition of double helical nucleic acids by proteins by Nadrian C. Seeman and others>[1976, pp. 804–808, major-groove arginine–guanine recognition model]{ref} <protein> geometry, hydration and phosphate contacts also affect sequence recognition; this local motif explains a preference rather than guaranteeing that every <arginine>-containing <protein> recognizes every G–C site.

= c
{parent=B5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

View the backbone along N–C$_\alpha$ to display $\phi=\angle(C'_{i-1},N_i,C_{\alpha i},C'_i)$ in a <Newman projection>. The front <amide> <nitrogen> carries the preceding <carbonyl> and H, while the back C$_\alpha$ carries the next <carbonyl>, H and side chain. Rotation changes the contacts among these groups. A corresponding view along C$_\alpha$–C$'$ displays $\psi$. The <peptide> C$'$–N bond itself is approximately planar and is not the freely rotating bond in either definition.

\Image[paper-1-newman.png]
{title=Newman views of backbone torsions and the contrasting restrictions of glycine and proline}

**<glycine> replaces the side chain by a second <hydrogen>.** Removing C$_\beta$ removes many contacts that exclude backbone arrangements for other residues, so its allowed $\phi,\psi$ region is much broader and includes substantial positive-$\phi$ space. Its alpha <carbon> is also <achiral>, so the steric map is approximately mirror-symmetric between positive and negative torsions in the simple isolated-residue model.

**<proline> closes a five-membered ring from C$_\alpha$ through three methylenes back to <nitrogen>.** In the N–C$_\alpha$ projection, the <nitrogen>'s side-chain arm and C$_\alpha$'s C$_\beta$ arm are tied together, preventing unrestricted relative rotation. Its $\phi$ is consequently confined near the usual negative-$60^\circ$ region rather than exploring the broad range of an acyclic <amino acid>. <proline> also lacks a backbone N–H donor when incorporated into an ordinary internal <peptide>. Ring closure restricts $\phi$ strongly, but does not freeze every backbone or ring degree of freedom. These differences are naturally represented by distinct <Ramachandran plots>; the original torsion/steric analysis established the basis for such comparisons.<Stereochemistry of polypeptide chain configurations by G. N. Ramachandran and others>[1963, pp. 95–99, backbone torsions and steric restrictions]{ref}
